WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.370
[Narrator] This is the AMC

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:03.870
on East Illinois Street in Chicago.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.827
They're playing 17 movies.

00:00:05.827 --> 00:00:07.900
But you actually have 21 options

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.040
because there's so many formats.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.490
Should you see "Toy
Story 4" in 3D? Digital?

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.328
Hang on, what's Dolby?

00:00:15.328 --> 00:00:17.720
And why does it cost 18 bucks?

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.095
Which one do you pick?

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.050
If you've bought a movie
ticket in the last decade,

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.810
you've seen this:

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.100
a bunch of different options,

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.960
each with dramatically different prices.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.540
The same movie is being screened,

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.240
but there are a lot of
ways it can be projected.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.650
In order to figure out why
there are so many formats,

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.420
we need to find out what
these actually mean.

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.970
It's more complicated than
the five names you see listed.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:47.660
Let's start with the basics.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.520
Standard, sometimes just
referred to as digital:

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.580
This is your average
movie-theater experience,

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.610
found anywhere from Regal to
a small independent theater.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.800
Then there's 3D, the one with the glasses,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.440
usually branded as RealD 3D.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.707
These projectors use polarized light

00:01:04.707 --> 00:01:06.830
to create the illusion of depth,

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.960
which supposedly makes
the film more immersive.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.100
But a lot of people just
find it disorienting.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.060
3D movies aren't as
popular as they once were.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.670
In fact, in 2018, the MPAA reported

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:23.670
a 20% year-over-year decrease
in the global 3D box office.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.860
All right, let's ditch those 3D glasses

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.670
and check out the newest
format to hit theaters,

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:29.648
Dolby.

00:01:29.648 --> 00:01:32.650
Dolby Digital Cinemas, made by Dolby,

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:34.750
can be found at AMC theaters.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.740
It uses dual 4K laser projectors.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.870
That's four times more resolution

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.130
than a standard theater projector.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.800
These projectors produce
super crisp images

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.710
with deep blacks and bright highlights,

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.940
at 500 times the contrast ratio

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.830
and twice the brightness
of a standard projector.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:56.610
Dolby is the best HDR you
can get in the theater.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.890
High dynamic range means
really bright highlights

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.170
and really dark blacks.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.300
Dolby Cinema images
appear less muddy or gray

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.670
compared to the traditional projectors.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.460
Overall, it's supposed to
create a more realistic image.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:15.250
And it reminds you, over
and over, with this video...

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.080
Ad announcer: Fire becomes fire.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.410
Sun becomes sun.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.120
[Narrator] Yeah, we get it.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.640
But the biggest difference with
Dolby is their sound system.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.750
They call it Dolby Atmos.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.350
All movie theaters use surround sound.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.250
That means using more speakers

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.677
so the viewer can hear
sounds from the direction

00:02:34.677 --> 00:02:37.108
they're supposed to be
coming from onscreen,

00:02:37.108 --> 00:02:39.530
like behind you, or to your right.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.960
But Dolby Atmos is like
super surround sound.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.390
It uses many more speakers

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.630
to completely surround the viewer.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.843
There are even speakers on the ceiling

00:02:48.843 --> 00:02:52.640
and transducers, which
produce bass under the seats.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.490
There's also a version of
Dolby that offers 3D movies.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.380
Thanks to the higher-quality projectors,

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.620
the image actually appears brighter

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.850
than with traditional 3D.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:05.573
And then there's IMAX.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:08.900
You've probably heard of IMAX,

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.920
because it's actually been
around since the '70s.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.120
But this can get a little confusing,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.380
because unlike Dolby, not
every IMAX theater is the same.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.050
Unique to IMAX is its huge screen,

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:25.627
which is bigger than any other
format, up to 40% larger,

00:03:25.627 --> 00:03:27.290
and it uses an aspect ratio

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.590
that's taller than other theaters.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:32.550
With some movies, this means
you're seeing more of the image

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:36.255
instead of black bars on the
top and bottom of the frame.

00:03:36.255 --> 00:03:39.920
The newest IMAX theaters
use dual 4K laser projectors

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.300
and have an upgraded sound system.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.850
But if you buy an IMAX ticket,

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.470
you might not be getting
your money's worth.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:47.790
Depending on your theater,

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:51.610
you may be getting what
some refer to as Lie-MAX.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.390
These Lie-MAX, ahem,
IMAX Experience theaters

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:59.960
have a smaller screen and
lower-resolution projectors.

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:01.870
It's not clear just
from buying your ticket

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:05.730
if your theater is IMAX
with laser or Lie-MAX,

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.510
but you can check this
website to find out.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:12.040
Like Dolby, some theaters
also offer IMAX 3D.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.760
But if you're walking
out of your IMAX movie

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.135
wondering why the screen
felt smaller than last time,

00:04:17.135 --> 00:04:19.290
you might spot one more logo.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.900
RPX is Regal's own
large-format experience,

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:25.193
offering bigger screens, newer projectors,

00:04:25.193 --> 00:04:27.320
and an updated sound system.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.780
That list probably sounds familiar by now.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.350
Basically, RPX will look and sound better

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.325
than a standard movie theater.

00:04:34.325 --> 00:04:37.900
But it's not as premium as
Dolby or IMAX with laser.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.862
Oh yeah, there's also 4DX,

00:04:39.862 --> 00:04:43.250
but, uh, that's its own thing.

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.940
This is a lot of information.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:48.320
But that's actually just digital.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.330
Some films offer special screenings

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:53.120
where you can view actual
film being projected.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.720
"Dunkirk" and "The Hateful Eight"

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.320
offered 70-millimeter screenings.

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:01.140
70mm is huge, more than six times bigger

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.630
than standard 35mm film.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.040
There's also 70mm IMAX,

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.900
which is how IMAX first started.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.080
Some moviegoers enjoy film projections

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.330
because it creates a more organic

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.100
and less sterile image
compared to digital.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.081
Besides 70mm, some theaters

00:05:18.081 --> 00:05:21.130
still offer 35mm film projections,

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:25.340
but these are usually for
special events, not new releases.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.770
So yeah, there's a lot
more to these screenings

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.420
than just their names.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:32.170
Here are a few takeaways
from all of those specs:

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:35.410
Bigger screens, newer projectors,
and larger sound systems

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.280
usually make a better viewing experience.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.930
But if IMAX and Dolby are both
bigger and higher-quality,

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:43.690
which is better?

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:45.210
Well, it depends.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.560
If you want the biggest
screen size you can get,

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.280
you want IMAX.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.490
With some screens over 80 feet wide,

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.300
IMAX is way bigger than Dolby.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.920
Just make sure it's IMAX with laser.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.702
But if you're an audiophile who has to get

00:05:58.702 --> 00:06:01.250
the best sound possible, go with Dolby.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:04.070
Dolby Atmos is noticeably more immersive

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.710
than traditional surround sound.

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:08.150
Dolby theaters are also usually newer,

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:10.860
so chances are it will
be better maintained.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.990
But both Dolby and IMAX
have better quality control

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:17.520
over the sound and image
than a standard theater.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.310
Dolby does offer a crisper, brighter image

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.770
with deeper blacks than IMAX.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:26.090
But both IMAX with laser
and Dolby look great.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:29.050
The average moviegoer probably
wouldn't notice a difference

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.720
without seeing these images side by side.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.750
But IMAX theaters also
have a taller screen,

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:37.460
so if you're watching a movie
like "Avengers: Endgame"

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:39.980
that was specifically shot for IMAX,

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:41.740
you'll see more of the image

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.960
instead of having black
bars on the top and bottom.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.830
But projection isn't everything.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.530
If you're going to watch a
three-hour "Avengers" movie,

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:50.730
you want to be comfortable.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.060
Certain theaters offer reclining seats,

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.480
food trays, or paired seats.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.650
These are a deal-breaker for some people.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.960
The specifics will vary
theater to theater,

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.240
but you can usually check online
before buying your ticket.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.720
Obviously, all of these
features won't come cheap.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.560
If you want to see a newer,
higher-quality format,

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.050
your ticket is going to cost more.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.040
Now, if all you wanted was to see a movie

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.540
on a hot summer day, this
might seem silly to you.

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:22.155
But some people are passionate
about how they watch a movie.

00:07:22.155 --> 00:07:23.205
And who can blame them?

00:07:23.205 --> 00:07:25.340
Movie tickets have hit an average price

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.781
of over $9 nationwide,

00:07:27.781 --> 00:07:30.946
and in big cities it's
even more expensive.

00:07:30.946 --> 00:07:32.200
If you're paying a bunch of money,

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.400
you want the best experience possible.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.670
All of these formats give
moviegoers more choice.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.880
If you just want to see a
comedy with some friends,

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:42.100
standard digital is probably fine.

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.920
But if you're seeing the big blockbuster

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:45.583
you've been waiting a year for,

00:07:45.583 --> 00:07:47.130
it might be worth the extra money

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.940
to see it in the biggest format available.

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:52.730
Different genres lend
themselves to different formats.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:54.460
And these formats give directors

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:57.150
and cinematographers more choices too.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.283
Christopher Nolan shot parts of "Dunkirk"

00:07:59.283 --> 00:08:00.883
with an IMAX camera,

00:08:00.883 --> 00:08:04.500
which made those exterior
shots feel large and epic,

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:07.860
while the interior shots
felt tight and confined.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.130
The taller IMAX aspect ratio was used

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:12.570
to add more impact to the story.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.010
And sound designers are
able to be more detailed

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:18.410
and creative when using a
system like Dolby Atmos.

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:21.600
All of these formats give
filmmakers the opportunity

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.800
to tell their story the
way they want to tell it.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.440
Of course, it helps the theaters too.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.300
Formats like Dolby and IMAX are a reason

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.610
to go to the theater
instead of watching at home.

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:37.600
But whether you want crispy
Dolby or grainy 35mm,

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.650
you've got a lot of options.

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:41.270
And that's a good thing.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.763
Imagine if 3D was the only
way you could see a movie.

